Now only 1082385 probes (core) are used per chip and the boxes in the
boxplot of the normalized and background-corrected data are PERFECTLY
aligned. (How can I add a plot here?)

By the way, now I find the implementation of the method
plotDensity.AffymetrixCelFile misleading... The default values for the
arguments are:
   function (this, subset = 1/2, types = NULL, ..., xlim = c(0, 16),
xlab = NULL, ylab = "density (integrates to one)", log = TRUE,
annotate = TRUE, verbose = FALSE)

subset =1/2 causes the method to plot a fraction of the date, here
half of the data. And since types=NULL, all the data is plottet (not
only the PM). So a standard call with plotDensity(anyCelFile) would
plot half of ALL probes (6553600/2) and would annotate the plot
correctly with "n=3276800". But the vertical annotation with the
chiptype is misleading in my opinion, because you would expect just
the probes defined by this cdf (e.g. core) to be plotted...?

You can avoid this by calling
   plotDensity(thisCelFile,ylim=c(0,1),types="pm",subset=NULL)

